FINAL: ### How Vector Databases Work
Vector databases are specialized systems designed to store high-dimensional numerical vectors and perform similarity searches at scale. Their internal architecture relies on three primary components: embedding generation, distance metrics, and Approximate Nearest Neighbor (ANN) indexing.

*   **Embedding Generation:** Raw unstructured data (text, images, audio) is transformed into dense numerical vectors via machine learning models. These embeddings capture semantic meaning, ensuring that similar concepts cluster together in high-dimensional space. Examples include OpenAI's `text-embedding-3-small` (1536 dimensions) and `text-embedding-3-large` (3072 dimensions), as well as open-source models like `sentence-transformers/all-MiniLM-L6-v2` (384 dimensions) (Source: [Vector Search Fundamentals](https://veduis.com/blog/vector-search-fundamentals/)).
*   **Distance Metrics:** These determine the similarity between vectors. 
    *   **Euclidean Distance ($L_2$):** Measures straight-line distance ($\sqrt{\sum (a_i - b_i)^2}$); lower values indicate higher similarity. It is often used in computer vision (Source: [Vector Search Fundamentals](https://veduis.com/blog/vector-search-fundamentals/)).
    *   **Cosine Similarity:** Measures the angle between vectors, ignoring magnitude. It ranges from -1 to 1 and is the default for text embeddings (Source: [Vector Search Fundamentals](https://veduis.com/blog/vector-search-fundamentals/)).
    *   **Dot Product:** The sum of element-wise products. When vectors are normalized to unit length, this becomes equivalent to cosine similarity (Source: [Vector Database Internals](https://yoichiozaki.github.io/en/blog/vector-database-internals)).
*   **Approximate Nearest Neighbor (ANN) Search:** Because exact "Flat" search requires $O(n)$ time complexity and struggles with the "curse of dimensionality," vector databases use ANN algorithms to trade a small, acceptable loss in accuracy for orders-of-magnitude faster query speeds (Source: [Vector Database Internals](https://yoichiozaki.github.io/en/blog/vector-database-internals)).

### Comparison of Indexing Approaches

| Indexing Approach | Mechanism | Key Parameters | Best Use Case |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **HNSW** | Multi-layer proximity graph; greedy traversal from sparse top to dense bottom layers. | `M` (links/node), `ef_construction`, `ef_search` | High recall/speed ratio; static or slowly changing datasets. |
| **IVF** | Partitions space into clusters (Voronoi cells) using k-means; searches only nearest clusters. | `nlist` (partitions), `nprobe` (clusters searched) | Frequently changing datasets; efficient for 100K to 10M+ vectors. |
| **PQ** | Lossy compression; splits vectors into subvectors and maps to codebook IDs. | `m` (subvectors), `nbits` (centroids) | Massive datasets; reduces memory footprint by 10x–50x. |

#### Detailed Indexing Analysis

**1. HNSW (Hierarchical Navigable Small World)**
*   **Structure:** Builds a multi-layer graph where each vector is a node. Upper layers are sparse for coarse navigation, while lower layers are dense for precision (Source: [Vector Index Types Explained](https://callsphere.ai/blog/vector-index-types-flat-ivf-hnsw-product-quantization)).
*   **Trade-offs:** Offers superior recall-to-latency ratios compared to IVF. However, it consumes high memory due to the graph structure and has slower, more expensive index construction (Source: [Vector search indexing](https://matthewpalma.dev/blog/vector-search-indexing-hnsw-ivf-recall-latency-tradeoffs)).

**2. IVF (Inverted File Index)**
*   **Structure:** Uses k-means to partition the vector space into `nlist` clusters. At query time, it only searches `nprobe` nearest clusters (Source: [Vector Index Types Explained](https://callsphere.ai/blog/vector-index-types-flat-ivf-hnsw-product-quantization)).
*   **Trade-offs:** Preferred for datasets that change frequently due to lower incremental insert costs. It requires a training step and does not automatically adapt to shifts in data distribution (Source: [Vector search indexing](https://matthewpalma.dev/blog/vector-search-indexing-hnsw-ivf-recall-latency-tradeoffs)).

**3. Product Quantization (PQ)**
*   **Structure:** A compression technique that splits high-dimensional vectors into `m` subvectors and quantizes each against a codebook (Source: [Vector Index Types Explained](https://callsphere.ai/blog/vector-index-types-flat-ivf-hnsw-product-quantization)).
*   **Trade-offs:** Dramatically reduces memory usage (e.g., a 1536-dimension float32 vector can shrink from 6KB to 64 bytes), but introduces approximation errors in distance calculations (Source: [Vector search indexing](https://matthewpalma.dev/blog/vector-search-indexing-hnsw-ivf-recall-latency-tradeoffs)).
*   **Hybridization:** These are often combined, such as **IVF+PQ**, to partition the search space while keeping memory usage low for billion-scale datasets (Source: [Vector Index Types Explained](https://callsphere.ai/blog/vector-index-types-flat-ivf-hnsw-product-quantization)).

### Caveats
- The provided sources focus on HNSW and IVF; other approaches like DiskANN or LSH are not covered.
