## How Vector Databases Work Internally

Vector databases are specialized storage systems designed to manage high-dimensional numerical vectors, which represent unstructured data such as text, images, or audio according to research from [Vector Search Fundamentals: Embeddings, Similarity Metrics ...](https://veduis.com/blog/vector-search-fundamentals/) (as of 2026-06-10). The process begins with embeddings, where machine learning models transform raw data into dense vectors—arrays of floating-point numbers—where semantic similarity is mapped to spatial proximity. In this high-dimensional space, items with similar meanings cluster together, allowing mathematical operations to determine relationship and similarity. According to source `https://veduis.com/blog/vector-search-fundamentals/`, embeddings are defined specifically as "dense numerical vectors that represent semantic meaning. Similar items have similar vectors."

To retrieve data, vector databases perform similarity searches by calculating the distance between a query vector and the stored vectors in the database. Three primary metrics are used:

* **Cosine Similarity:** Measures the cosine of the angle between two vectors while ignoring magnitude, making it the most common choice for text embeddings as reported by [Vector Search Fundamentals: Embeddings, Similarity Metrics ...](https://veduis.com/blog/vector-search-fundamentals/) (as of 2026-06-10). Its source quote notes it "Measures angle between vectors, ignoring magnitude. ... Most common for text embeddings."
* **Euclidean Distance (L2):** Calculates the straight-line distance between two points, which is useful when magnitude is significant, such as in computer vision embeddings according to [Vector Search Fundamentals: Embeddings, Similarity Metrics ...](https://veduis.com/blog/vector-search-fundamentals/) (as of 2026-06-10). Its source quote specifies "Straight-line distance between vectors. ... When magnitude matters. ... Computer vision embeddings."
* **Dot Product:** Calculates the sum of element-wise products, serving as a computationally efficient approach that is equivalent to Cosine Similarity when vectors are normalized, per [Vector Search Fundamentals: Embeddings, Similarity Metrics ...](https://veduis.com/blog/vector-search-fundamentals/) (as of 2026-06-10). The source notes it is a "Simple sum of element-wise products. ... When vectors are normalized (equivalent to cosine)."

Because performing an exact nearest neighbor search across millions of vectors is computationally prohibitive, vector databases employ Approximate Nearest Neighbor (ANN) algorithms to achieve millisecond latency at the cost of slight accuracy loss. Common indexing strategies include HNSW (Hierarchical Navigable Small World), which builds a layered, graph-based structure allowing for efficient "greedy" traversal to the most similar vectors; the source [Vector Search Fundamentals: Embeddings, Similarity Metrics ...](https://veduis.com/blog/vector-search-fundamentals/) identifies HNSW as a "Graph-based algorithm. Most popular for production" (as of 2026-06-10). Another approach is IVF (Inverted File Index), which partitions the vector space into clusters (Voronoi cells); at query time, the system identifies the nearest cluster centroids and searches only within those relevant partitions, functioning as a "Clustering-based approach" that searches "only vectors in those clusters" according to [Vector Search Fundamentals: Embeddings, Similarity Metrics ...](https://veduis.com/blog/vector-search-fundamentals/) (as of 2026-06-10).

To optimize for memory and performance at scale, databases often use compression techniques like Product Quantization (PQ). As detailed by [Vector Search Fundamentals: Embeddings, Similarity Metrics ...](https://veduis.com/blog/vector-search-fundamentals/) (as of 2026-06-10), PQ breaks vectors into smaller sub-vectors and quantizes them into codebooks by compressing vectors to: 1. Split vector into sub-vectors, and 2. Quantize each sub-vector to a codebook. This significantly reduces memory footprint while accelerating distance calculations. These indexing and compression methods allow modern vector databases to handle massive, high-dimensional datasets that would be impossible to process using traditional keyword-based indexing.

## Detailed Comparison of Vector Indexing Approaches

Vector similarity search relies heavily on Approximate Nearest Neighbor (ANN) algorithms because exact exhaustive flat k-NN search ($O(N \cdot D)$) is computationally prohibitive for large datasets exceeding millions of high-dimensional vectors, as noted by [Vector Database Indexing Guide: HNSW vs IVF-PQ vs ScaNN](https://www.nexprotools.com/blog/vector-database-indexing-hnsw-pq-ann-search-guide) (as of 2026-09-01). The three foundational approaches used in modern vector databases are HNSW, IVF, and Product Quantization (PQ).

### Hierarchical Navigable Small World (HNSW)
HNSW constructs multi-layered proximity graphs where upper layers act as sparse long-distance highways for rapid logarithmic routing, and the base layer ($l_0$) contains dense local Delaunay-like clustering. According to [Vector Database Indexing Guide: HNSW vs IVF-PQ vs ScaNN](https://www.nexprotools.com/blog/vector-database-indexing-hnsw-pq-ann-search-guide) (as of 2026-09-01), HNSW achieves top-tier recall (>98% Recall@10) with sub-3ms query latencies (specifically sub-2ms according to its quote: "HNSW achieves top-tier Recall@10 (> 98%) with sub-2ms latency, at the cost of 1.5x–2.5x RAM overhead"). 

Its primary parameters include:
* **$M$:** The maximum bidirectional connections per node, typically 16 to 64.
* **$efConstruction$:** The size of the dynamic candidate list during graph building.
* **$efSearch$:** The candidate list size at query time (noted as HNSW's query-time recall/latency knob by [Vector search indexing: HNSW, IVF, and the recall/latency trade-offs that actually matter in production](https://matthewpalma.dev/blog/vector-search-indexing-hnsw-ivf-recall-latency-tradeoffs)).

HNSW natively supports real-time dynamic node insertions without a full index rebuild, but carries a high memory overhead, typically requiring 1.5x to 2.5x the raw vector storage in RAM (~7.2 GB per 1 million 1536-dimensional vectors).

### Inverted File Index (IVF)
Inverted File Index (IVF) partitions the high-dimensional vector space into $K$ Voronoi cells using K-Means clustering, as supported by [Vector Database Indexing Guide: HNSW vs IVF-PQ vs ScaNN](https://www.nexprotools.com/blog/vector-database-indexing-hnsw-pq-ann-search-guide) ("Inverted File indexes partition vector space into K Voronoi cells"). During a search query, the system evaluates only a subset of these clusters controlled by parameters like $nprobe$ (or $nlist$), reducing search complexity from linear to sub-linear ($O(nprobe \cdot N / nlist)$). 

According to [Vector search indexing: HNSW, IVF, and the recall/latency trade-offs that actually matter in production](https://matthewpalma.dev/blog/vector-search-indexing-hnsw-ivf-recall-latency-tradeoffs), IVF clusters the vector space during index build, and `nprobe` (probes) serves as the main recall/latency knob at query time (increasing `nprobe` improves recall but raises latency). IVF drastically reduces search scope and memory requirements compared to flat indexes, but it requires periodic retraining of Voronoi centroids and does not natively support real-time streaming updates without rebuilding or re-clustering the index.

### Product Quantization (PQ)
Product Quantization (PQ) is a lossy compression technique designed to reduce the memory footprint of massive vector datasets that cannot fit entirely in RAM. As outlined by [Vector Database Indexing Guide: HNSW vs IVF-PQ vs ScaNN](https://www.nexprotools.com/blog/vector-database-indexing-hnsw-pq-ann-search-guide) (as of 2026-09-01), PQ divides high-dimensional vectors into $M$ sub-vectors (e.g., $M = 64$ sub-vectors of dimension 24 for a $D = 1,536$ vector) quantized against 256 codebook centroids ($K^* = 256$) generated via K-Means, compressing 1536-dimensional float32 vectors by 95%. 

[Vector search indexing: HNSW, IVF, and the recall/latency trade-offs that actually matter in production](https://matthewpalma.dev/blog/vector-search-indexing-hnsw-ivf-recall-latency-tradeoffs) notes that PQ replaces each subvector with the ID of its nearest codebook centroid, effectively compressing data at the cost of approximating distances rather than computing them exactly. This introduces a "quantization tax"—a reduction in recall accuracy compared to full-precision search.

### Summary Comparison of Trade-Offs
As discussed in [Vector Search Indexing: HNSW vs IVF-PQ and the Latency Trade-Off](https://mehaisi.com/blog/vector-search-hnsw-vs-ivf-pq) and [Vector search indexing: HNSW, IVF, and the recall/latency trade-offs that actually matter in production](https://matthewpalma.dev/blog/vector-search-indexing-hnsw-ivf-recall-latency-tradeoffs):
* **HNSW:** Fast, highly accurate, and incredibly memory-hungry. It is preferred when memory is available and sub-millisecond latency/high recall are required on static or slowly changing datasets, though its build time is relatively slow and computationally expensive due to graph-wiring requirements per insertion.
* **IVF & IVF-PQ:** IVF partitions space to reduce search scope, while PQ relies on compression mechanisms to drastically cut down the memory footprint (e.g., reducing thousands of bytes down to a few bytes per vector). IVF-PQ is mandatory for massive, billion-scale datasets where memory costs must be controlled, accepting lower recall and higher latency as a trade-off. 
* **Production Pipelines:** Production architectures frequently combine these approaches (such as IVF-PQ for massive scale) or incorporate re-ranking steps (using a cross-encoder) to retrieve a larger candidate set efficiently via indexing and then perform exact re-ranking to mitigate the accuracy losses of lossy techniques like PQ.

