# How Vector Databases Work and Indexing Approaches

Vector databases are specialized storage systems designed to manage high-dimensional numerical vectors, which represent unstructured data such as text, images, or audio. The process begins with embeddings, where machine learning models transform raw data into dense vectors—arrays of floating-point numbers—where semantic similarity is mapped to spatial proximity ([Vector Search Fundamentals: Embeddings, Similarity Metrics ...](https://veduis.com/blog/vector-search-fundamentals/)). In this high-dimensional space, items with similar meanings cluster together, allowing mathematical operations to determine relationship and similarity.

## Similarity Search Operations and Distance Metrics

To retrieve data, vector databases perform similarity searches by calculating the distance between a query vector and the stored vectors in the database. Three primary metrics are used:

* **Cosine Similarity:** Measures the cosine of the angle between two vectors, ignoring magnitude. As noted by [Vector Search Fundamentals: Embeddings, Similarity Metrics ...](https://veduis.com/blog/vector-search-fundamentals/), this makes it the most common choice for normalized text embeddings.
* **Euclidean Distance (L2):** Calculates the straight-line distance between two points. According to [Vector Search Fundamentals: Embeddings, Similarity Metrics ...](https://veduis.com/blog/vector-search-fundamentals/), this metric is useful when magnitude is significant, such as in computer vision embeddings.
* **Dot Product:** Calculates the sum of element-wise products. It is computationally efficient and equivalent to Cosine Similarity when vectors are normalized ([Vector Search Fundamentals: Embeddings, Similarity Metrics ...](https://veduis.com/blog/vector-search-fundamentals/)).

## Vector Indexing Approaches: HNSW, IVF, and Product Quantization

Because performing an exact exhaustive flat $k$-NN search ($O(N \cdot D)$) across millions of vectors is computationally prohibitive at scale (>1M vectors), vector databases employ Approximate Nearest Neighbor (ANN) algorithms to achieve millisecond latency at the cost of slight accuracy loss ([Vector Database Indexing Guide: HNSW vs IVF-PQ vs ScaNN](https://www.nexprotools.com/blog/vector-database-indexing-hnsw-pq-ann-search-guide)). The foundational indexing strategies include:

* **Hierarchical Navigable Small World (HNSW):** Constructs multi-layered proximity graphs where upper layers act as sparse long-distance highways for rapid logarithmic routing (or greedy hops), and the base layer ($l_0$) contains fine-grained local Delaunay-like clustering ([Vector Database Indexing Guide: HNSW vs IVF-PQ vs ScaNN](https://www.nexprotools.com/blog/vector-database-indexing-hnsw-pq-ann-search-guide)). HNSW achieves top-tier recall (>98% Recall@10) with sub-3ms query latencies ([Vector Database Indexing Guide: HNSW vs IVF-PQ vs ScaNN](https://www.nexprotools.com/blog/vector-database-indexing-hnsw-pq-ann-search-guide)). Its primary parameters include $M$ (the maximum bidirectional connections per node, typically 16 to 64), $efConstruction$ (size of the dynamic candidate list during graph building), and $efSearch$ (candidate list size at query time) ([Vector Database System Design: HNSW, IVF, and Product Quantization](https://www.designgurus.io/blog/vector-database-hnsw-ivf)). HNSW natively supports real-time dynamic node insertions without a full index rebuild, but carries a high memory overhead, typically requiring 1.5x to 2.5x the raw vector storage in RAM (~7.2 GB per 1 million 1536-dimensional vectors) ([Vector Database System Design: HNSW, IVF, and Product Quantization](https://www.designgurus.io/blog/vector-database-hnsw-ivf)).
* **Inverted File Index (IVF):** Partitions the high-dimensional vector space into $K$ Voronoi cells using K-Means clustering ([Vector Database System Design: HNSW, IVF, and Product Quantization](https://www.designgurus.io/blog/vector-database-hnsw-ivf)). During a search query, the system evaluates only a subset of these clusters ($nprobe$ or $nlist$), reducing search complexity from linear to sub-linear ($O(nprobe \cdot N / nlist)$) ([Vector Database System Design: HNSW, IVF, and Product Quantization](https://www.designgurus.io/blog/vector-database-hnsw-ivf)). IVF drastically reduces search scope and memory requirements compared to flat indexes, but it requires periodic retraining of Voronoi centroids and does not natively support real-time streaming updates without rebuilding or re-clustering the index ([Vector Database System Design: HNSW, IVF, and Product Quantization](https://www.designgurus.io/blog/vector-database-hnsw-ivf)).
* **Product Quantization (PQ):** A lossy compression technique designed to reduce the memory footprint of massive vector datasets that cannot fit entirely in RAM ([Vector Database System Design: HNSW, IVF, and Product Quantization](https://www.designgurus.io/blog/vector-database-hnsw-ivf)). PQ splits a high-dimensional vector (e.g., $D = 1,536$) into $M$ smaller sub-vectors (e.g., $M = 64$ sub-vectors of dimension 24) ([Vector Database System Design: HNSW, IVF, and Product Quantization](https://www.designgurus.io/blog/vector-database-hnsw-ivf)). Each sub-vector is quantized against a codebook of $K^* = 256$ centroids generated via K-Means and represented as an 8-bit integer index (1 byte) ([Vector Database System Design: HNSW, IVF, and Product Quantization](https://www.designgurus.io/blog/vector-database-hnsw-ivf)). This compresses 1536-dimensional float32 vectors (6,144 bytes) down to 64 bytes—a 95%+ memory reduction ([Vector Database System Design: HNSW, IVF, and Product Quantization](https://www.designgurus.io/blog/vector-database-hnsw-ivf)). IVF and PQ are frequently combined (IVF-PQ) to cluster vectors into Voronoi cells while compressing the vectors within those cells, enabling billion-scale vector databases on constrained memory budgets ([Vector Database System Design: HNSW, IVF, and Product Quantization](https://www.designgurus.io/blog/vector-database-hnsw-ivf)).

## Comparison of Trade-Offs: HNSW, IVF, and PQ

Vector indexing strategies primarily balance the trade-off between search speed (latency), search quality (recall), and resource utilization (memory and build time) ([Vector Search Indexing: HNSW vs IVF-PQ and the Latency Trade-Off](https://mehaisi.com/blog/vector-search-hnsw-vs-ivf-pq)).

* **HNSW Trade-Offs:** HNSW is fast, highly accurate, and incredibly memory-hungry, making it widely favored for static or slowly changing datasets where memory is available and sub-millisecond latency/high recall are required ([Vector Search Indexing: HNSW vs IVF-PQ and the Latency Trade-Off](https://mehaisi.com/blog/vector-search-hnsw-vs-ivf-pq), [Vector search indexing: HNSW, IVF, and the recall/latency trade-offs that actually matter in production](https://matthewpalma.dev/blog/vector-search-indexing-hnsw-ivf-recall-latency-tradeoffs)). Its build time is relatively slow and computationally expensive because each insertion requires finding and wiring the new node into the existing multi-layer graph structure ([Vector Search Indexing: HNSW vs IVF-PQ and the Latency Trade-Off](https://mehaisi.com/blog/vector-search-hnsw-vs-ivf-pq), [Vector search indexing: HNSW, IVF, and the recall/latency trade-offs that actually matter in production](https://matthewpalma.dev/blog/vector-search-indexing-hnsw-ivf-recall-latency-tradeoffs)). Query-time performance and recall are controlled by the `ef_search` parameter ([Vector search indexing: HNSW, IVF, and the recall/latency trade-offs that actually matter in production](https://matthewpalma.dev/blog/vector-search-indexing-hnsw-ivf-recall-latency-tradeoffs)).
* **IVF Trade-Offs:** IVF is generally faster to build than HNSW and handles incremental updates more easily, as it does not require complex graph re-wiring ([Vector Search Indexing: HNSW vs IVF-PQ and the Latency Trade-Off](https://mehaisi.com/blog/vector-search-hnsw-vs-ivf-pq)). However, its performance depends on the quality of data partitioning, and at query time, the `nprobe` parameter acts as the main recall/latency knob (increasing `nprobe` improves recall but raises latency) ([Vector Search Indexing: HNSW vs IVF-PQ and the Latency Trade-Off](https://mehaisi.com/blog/vector-search-hnsw-vs-ivf-pq), [Vector search indexing: HNSW, IVF, and the recall/latency trade-offs that actually matter in production](https://matthewpalma.dev/blog/vector-search-indexing-hnsw-ivf-recall-latency-tradeoffs)).
* **PQ Trade-Offs:** Product Quantization relies on lossy compression by replacing sub-vectors with codebook centroid IDs, introducing a 'quantization tax'—a reduction in recall accuracy compared to full-precision search in exchange for a drastically smaller memory footprint ([Vector Search Indexing: HNSW vs IVF-PQ and the Latency Trade-Off](https://mehaisi.com/blog/vector-search-hnsw-vs-ivf-pq), [Vector search indexing: HNSW, IVF, and the recall/latency trade-offs that actually matter in production](https://matthewpalma.dev/blog/vector-search-indexing-hnsw-ivf-recall-latency-tradeoffs)). IVF-PQ is mandatory for massive datasets where memory costs must be controlled, accepting lower recall and higher latency as a trade-off ([Vector Search Indexing: HNSW vs IVF-PQ and the Latency Trade-Off](https://mehaisi.com/blog/vector-search-hnsw-vs-ivf-pq)).

Production pipelines often combine these approaches with re-ranking (e.g., using a cross-encoder) to mitigate the accuracy losses of lossy indexing techniques like PQ, retrieving a larger candidate set efficiently and then performing an exact re-ranking ([Vector Search Indexing: HNSW vs IVF-PQ and the Latency Trade-Off](https://mehaisi.com/blog/vector-search-hnsw-vs-ivf-pq)).

Caveats:
- The sources provide qualitative trade-offs rather than a standardized, quantitative benchmark comparison across all metrics.
- The exact 'as of' date for technical content varies by source (ranging mid-to-late 2026).

