FINAL: This report explains the architecture and operational workflow of vector databases, which are designed to store and query high-dimensional embeddings for semantic search. It details the ingestion and retrieval processes, compares the primary indexing strategies—Flat, IVF, and HNSW—and analyzes the performance trade-offs between latency, recall, and memory consumption. You can read this as a comprehensive guide to understanding how these systems manage data at scale and how to tune them for specific application requirements.

## How Vector Databases Work: The Core Workflow

Vector databases are specialized storage engines designed to store, index, and query high-dimensional numerical vectors, known as embeddings. Unlike traditional databases that rely on keyword matching, vector databases enable semantic search by representing data as coordinates in a high-dimensional space where distance corresponds to semantic similarity, according to a deep dive by [Deep Dive into Vector Databases: Indexing, Embeddings, and Querying](https://blog.bytedoodle.com/vector-databases-data-storage-querying-and-embeddings/). Furthermore, as noted by [Embeddings and Vector Databases — The Complete Guide](https://tutorialq.com/ai/ml/embeddings-and-vector-databases), vector databases store data specifically as dense numerical vectors that capture semantic meaning and complex relationships.

The core workflow begins with embedding ingestion. Raw data such as text, images, and audio is converted into fixed-length floating-point arrays by an external embedding model (such as OpenAI text-embedding-3, BGE-M3, or Sentence-BERT). This process is deterministic, meaning identical inputs always produce identical output vectors. This characteristic is critical because search works by comparing query embeddings to stored embeddings, and they must come from the same model to be comparable, as detailed in [How Vector Databases Work: Embeddings, Indexes, and Similarity Search](https://www.autolearningagents.com/vector-databases/how-vector-databases-work.php) (dated 2026-07-15). 

To maintain retrieval precision, data is often chunked into smaller, focused segments before embedding, as models have token limits and long documents can dilute semantic focus ([Embeddings and Vector Databases — The Complete Guide](https://tutorialq.com/ai/ml/embeddings-and-vector-databases)). Specifically, a recommended solution is to split documents into focused chunks that each cover one idea, embedding each chunk separately. During storage, the database saves the vector alongside associated metadata, which enables filtering operations separate from the vector search ([Deep Dive into Vector Databases: Indexing, Embeddings, and Querying](https://blog.bytedoodle.com/vector-databases-data-storage-querying-and-embeddings/)).

To ensure efficient retrieval at scale, vector databases use specialized index structures. While brute-force searching (Flat index) is accurate, it is computationally expensive and scales linearly with the dataset size. Consequently, most production databases utilize Approximate Nearest Neighbor (ANN) indexes. HNSW (Hierarchical Navigable Small World) is a dominant choice—noted as the dominant index type used by systems like Qdrant, Weaviate, pgvector, and ChromaDB ([How Vector Databases Work: Embeddings, Indexes, and Similarity Search](https://www.autolearningagents.com/vector-databases/how-vector-databases-work.php))—which creates a multi-layer graph where a query traverses from coarse, sparse layers to dense, detailed layers. Another method is IVF (Inverted File Index), which partitions data into clusters to limit the search space to relevant regions ([Deep Dive into Vector Databases: Indexing, Embeddings, and Querying](https://blog.bytedoodle.com/vector-databases-data-storage-querying-and-embeddings/)).

Similarity search represents the final phase of the workflow. When a query is received, it is converted into a vector using the same model used for ingestion. The database then calculates the distance between the query vector and stored vectors using metrics such as cosine similarity, dot product, or Euclidean distance (L2). Text embeddings almost universally use cosine similarity because it is scale-invariant and the most intuitive for semantic comparison, according to [How Vector Databases Work: Embeddings, Indexes, and Similarity Search](https://www.autolearningagents.com/vector-databases/how-vector-databases-work.php). Metadata filters are also applied either pre-search (ensuring exact matches but potentially slowing performance) or post-search (faster but risking fewer results), and the database finally returns the top-k nearest neighbors based on the chosen similarity metric ([Deep Dive into Vector Databases: Indexing, Embeddings, and Querying](https://blog.bytedoodle.com/vector-databases-data-storage-querying-and-embeddings/)).

