# How Vector Databases Work: Core Workflow and Architecture

Vector databases are specialized storage engines designed to store, index, and query high-dimensional numerical vectors known as embeddings. Unlike traditional databases that rely on strict keyword matching, vector databases enable semantic search by representing data as coordinates in a high-dimensional space, where distance corresponds to semantic similarity [Deep Dive into Vector Databases: Indexing, Embeddings, and Querying](https://blog.bytedoodle.com/vector-databases-data-storage-querying-and-embeddings/). According to findings, vector databases store data as dense numerical vectors—also called embeddings—that capture semantic meaning and complex relationships.

## 1. Embedding Ingestion and Preparation
The workflow begins with embedding ingestion, where raw data such as text, images, and audio is converted into fixed-length floating-point arrays by an external embedding model. Examples of such models include OpenAI text-embedding-3, BGE-M3, and Sentence-BERT [Deep Dive into Vector Databases: Indexing, Embeddings, and Querying](https://blog.bytedoodle.com/vector-databases-data-storage-querying-and-embeddings/). This process is entirely deterministic; as noted in sources, the same input always produces the same output vector [How Vector Databases Work: Embeddings, Indexes, and Similarity Search](https://www.autolearningagents.com/vector-databases/how-vector-databases-work.php) (as of July 15, 2026). 

Because language models have token limits and long documents can dilute semantic focus, data is frequently split into smaller, focused chunks before embedding to maintain retrieval precision [Embeddings and Vector Databases — The Complete Guide](https://tutorialq.com/ai/ml/embeddings-and-vector-databases). As outlined in source material, users should split documents into focused chunks that each cover one topic or idea and embed each chunk separately. During storage, the database saves the vector alongside associated metadata, which enables filtering operations separate from the vector search [Deep Dive into Vector Databases: Indexing, Embeddings, and Querying](https://blog.bytedoodle.com/vector-databases-data-storage-querying-and-embeddings/). Furthermore, embeddings must be generated using the same model for both ingestion and query to be comparable, because search works by comparing query embeddings to stored embeddings [How Vector Databases Work: Embeddings, Indexes, and Similarity Search](https://www.autolearningagents.com/vector-databases/how-vector-databases-work.php) (as of July 15, 2026).

## 2. Indexing Structures for Scale
To ensure efficient retrieval at scale, vector databases use specialized index structures. While a brute-force search (using a Flat index) is accurate, it is computationally expensive and scales linearly with dataset size [Deep Dive into Vector Databases: Indexing, Embeddings, and Querying](https://blog.bytedoodle.com/vector-databases-data-storage-querying-and-embeddings/]. Consequently, most production databases utilize Approximate Nearest Neighbor (ANN) indexes [Deep Dive into Vector Databases: Indexing, Embeddings, and Querying](https://blog.bytedoodle.com/vector-databases-data-storage-querying-and-embeddings/). 

The dominant index type is HNSW (Hierarchical Navigable Small World), which is used by systems such as Qdrant, Weaviate, pgvector, and ChromaDB [How Vector Databases Work: Embeddings, Indexes, and Similarity Search](https://www.autolearningagents.com/vector-databases/how-vector-databases-work.php) (as of July 15, 2026). HNSW builds a multi-layer graph where a query traverses from coarse, sparse layers down to dense, detailed layers [Deep Dive into Vector Databases: Indexing, Embeddings, and Querying](https://blog.bytedoodle.com/vector-databases-data-storage-querying-and-embeddings/). Other methods include IVF (Inverted File Index), which partitions data into clusters to limit the search space to relevant regions [Deep Dive into Vector Databases: Indexing, Embeddings, and Querying](https://blog.bytedoodle.com/vector-databases-data-storage-querying-and-embeddings/).

## 3. Similarity Search and Query Execution
Similarity search represents the final phase of the workflow. When a query is received, it is converted into a vector using the exact same model used during ingestion [Deep Dive into Vector Databases: Indexing, Embeddings, and Querying](https://blog.bytedoodle.com/vector-databases-data-storage-querying-and-embeddings/). The database calculates the distance between the query vector and stored vectors using metrics such as cosine similarity (measuring the angle between vectors), dot product, or Euclidean distance (L2) [Deep Dive-into Vector Databases: Indexing, Embeddings, and Querying](https://blog.bytedoodle.com/vector-databases-data-storage-querying-and-embeddings/). Text embeddings almost universally use cosine similarity because it is scale-invariant and the most intuitive choice for semantic comparison [How Vector Databases Work: Embeddings, Indexes, and Similarity Search](https://www.autolearningagents.com/vector-databases/how-vector-databases-work.php) (as of July 15, 2026).

Metadata filters are applied either pre-search (ensuring exact matches but potentially slowing down performance) or post-search (faster, but risking fewer total results) [Deep Dive into Vector Databases: Indexing, Embeddings, and Querying](https://blog.bytedoodle.com/vector-databases-data-storage-querying-and-embeddings/). Finally, the database returns the top-k nearest neighbors based on the chosen similarity metric [Deep Dive into Vector Databases: Indexing, Embeddings, and Querying](https://blog.bytedoodle.com/vector-databases-data-storage-querying-and-embeddings/).

# Detailed Comparison of Vector Indexing Approaches

Vector database indexing is essential for enabling fast similarity searches. The three primary approaches—Flat, IVF, and HNSW—represent distinct trade-offs between search speed, accuracy (recall), memory consumption, and update flexibility [Vector Index Types: Flat, HNSW, IVF, PQ Explained!](https://www.dataproduct.net/vector-databases/10-vector-index-types-explained-flat-hnsw-ivf-pq.html).

## 1. Flat Indexing (Brute-Force Search)
Flat indexing performs an exhaustive "brute-force" search by comparing a query vector against every single vector stored in the dataset [Vector Similarity Search Explained: Flat Index, IVF, and HNSW](https://medium.com/@subhamnayak_76/vector-similarity-search-explained-flat-index-ivf-and-hnsw-d7a145055d41). 
* **Recall / Accuracy:** Provides 100% recall and perfect accuracy because no data points are skipped [Vector Index Types: Flat, HNSW, IVF, PQ Explained!](https://www.dataproduct.net/vector-databases/10-vector-index-types-explained-flat-hnsw-ivf-pq.html).
* **Performance & Scalability:** Computationally expensive and scales linearly with the number of vectors, making it poorly suited for large-scale datasets [Vector Index Types: Flat, HNSW, IVF, PQ Explained!](https://www.dataproduct.net/vector-databases/10-vector-index-types-explained-flat-hnsw-ivf-pq.html).
* **Use Case:** Remains the ideal baseline for smaller datasets where perfect accuracy is non-negotiable [Vector Index Types: Flat, HNSW, IVF, PQ Explained!](https://www.dataproduct.net/vector-databases/10-vector-index-types-explained-flat-hnsw-ivf-pq.html).

## 2. Inverted File Index (IVF)
IVF partitions the high-dimensional vector space into clusters using centroids, which are typically calculated via K-means clustering (forming structures like Voronoi cells) [Vector Index Types: Flat, HNSW, IVF, PQ Explained!](https://www.dataproduct.net/vector-databases/10-vector-index-types-explained-flat-hnsw-ivf-pq.html), [Vector search indexing: HNSW, IVF, and the recall/latency trade-offs](https://matthewpalma.dev/blog/vector-search-indexing-hnsw-ivf-recall-latency-tradeoffs).
* **Search Mechanism:** During a search, the system identifies the nearest centroids to the query vector and only searches the vectors contained within those specific partitions, drastically reducing the required number of comparisons [Vector Index Types: Flat, HNSW, IVF, PQ Explained!](https://www.dataproduct.net/vector-databases/10-vector-index-types-explained-flat-hnsw-ivf-pq.html), [HNSW vs. IVF-Flat: The Battle for Scalable Vector Search](https://notes.suhaib.in/docs/tech/latest/hnsw-vs-ivf-flat-the-battle-for-scalable-vector-search/).
* **Memory & Updates:** IVF-Flat is far leaner and more memory-efficient than HNSW, requiring storage only for centroids and partitioned vectors [HNSW vs. IVF-Flat: The Battle for Scalable Vector Search](https://notes.suhaib.in/docs/tech/latest/hnsw-vs-ivf-flat-the-battle-for-scalable-vector-search/). Its cheaper build cost and simpler incremental-insert story make it a strong default when datasets change frequently and incremental build costs matter more than peak recall [Vector search indexing: HNSW, IVF, and the recall/latency trade-offs](https://matthewpalma.dev/blog/vector-search-indexing-hnsw-ivf-recall-latency-tradeoffs).
* **Parameters:** Performance relies heavily on tuning `nlist` (the number of partitions) and `nprobe` (the number of partitions searched at query time) [Vector Index Tuning: HNSW, IVF & Product Quantization](https://appscale.blog/en/blog/vector-index-tuning-hnsw-ivf-product-quantization-recall-latency-2026).

## 3. Hierarchical Navigable Small World (HNSW)
HNSW is a graph-based indexing method that builds a multi-layered graph where vectors act as nodes and connections represent proximity [HNSW vs. IVF-Flat: The Battle for Scalable Vector Search](https://notes.suhaib.in/docs/tech/latest/hnsw-vs-ivf-flat-the-battle-for-scalable-vector-search/).
* **Search Mechanism:** Queries traverse the graph starting from sparse top-level layers down to dense bottom-layer graphs, achieving logarithmic-time search performance and high recall accuracy [Vector Index Types: Flat, HNSW, IVF, PQ Explained!](https://www.dataproduct.net/vector-databases/10-vector-index-types-explained-flat-hnsw-ivf-pq.html), [HNSW vs. IVF-Flat: The Battle for Scalable Vector Search](https://notes.suhaib.in/docs/tech/latest/hnsw-vs-ivf-flat-the-battle-for-scalable-vector-search/).
* **Strengths & Weaknesses:** Delivers best-in-class raw latency for high-recall workloads [Vector search at 100M+ scale: IVF, HNSW, DiskANN (2026 update)](https://contentwave.net/article/enterprise-vector-search-at-100m-scale-updated-tradeoffs-june-2026). However, it is significantly more memory-intensive than IVF-Flat because it must store complex graph connections alongside vectors, and updating the graph with new data is computationally expensive [HNSW vs. IVF-Flat: The Battle for Scalable Vector Search](https://notes.suhaib.in/docs/tech/latest/hnsw-vs-ivf-flat-the-battle-for-scalable-vector-search/), [Vector search at 100M+ scale: IVF, HNSW, DiskANN (2026 update)](https://contentwave.net/article/enterprise-vector-search-at-100m-scale-updated-tradeoffs-june-2026).
* **Parameters:** Governed by parameters like `m` (number of bi-directional links per node), `ef_construction` (thoroughness during index build), and `ef_search` (query-time knob trading latency for recall) [Vector Index Tuning: HNSW, IVF & Product Quantization](https://appscale.blog/en/blog/vector-index-tuning-hnsw-ivf-product-quantization-recall-latency-2026).

# Performance Trade-Offs: Latency, Recall, and Memory Management

Vector indexing fundamentally balances trade-offs among search latency, recall accuracy, and memory footprint [Vector search indexing: HNSW, IVF, and the recall/latency trade-offs](https://matthewpalma.dev/blog/vector-search-indexing-hnsw-ivf-recall-latency-tradeoffs). Approximate Nearest Neighbor (ANN) indexes trade a small, tunable amount of recall for orders-of-magnitude faster search performance compared to exhaustive brute-force methods [Vector search indexing: HNSW, IVF, and the recall/latency trade-offs](https://matthewpalma.dev/blog/vector-search-indexing-hnsw-ivf-recall-latency-tradeoffs) (as of August 19, 2026).

## 1. Quantization and Memory Optimization
At scale, raw float32 embeddings require massive amounts of RAM. To mitigate this, vector databases employ quantization techniques, most notably **Product Quantization (PQ)** [Vector search indexing: HNSW, IVF, and the recall/latency trade-offs](https://matthewpalma.dev/blog/vector-search-indexing-hnsw-ivf-recall-latency-tradeoffs). PQ splits each high-dimensional vector into subvectors and replaces each subvector with the identifier of its nearest codebook centroid [Vector search indexing: HNSW, IVF, and the recall/latency trade-offs](https://matthewpalma.dev/blog/vector-search-indexing-hnsw-ivf-recall-latency-tradeoffs). This effectively compresses a 1,536-dimension float32 embedding into a handful of bytes, though it comes at the cost of distance approximation accuracy that compounds with ANN recall loss [Vector search indexing: HNSW, IVF, and the recall/latency trade-offs](https://matthewpalma.dev/blog/vector-search-indexing-hnsw-ivf-recall-latency-tradeoffs) (as of August 19, 2026).

## 2. Enterprise Scale and Tiered Storage (DiskANN)
For massive enterprise datasets containing 100 million or more vectors, pure in-memory indexes face severe RAM limitations [Vector search at 100M+ scale: IVF, HNSW, DiskANN (2026 update)](https://contentwave.net/article/enterprise-vector-search-at-100m-scale-updated-tradeoffs-june-2026) (as of September 26, 2026). Modern architectures increasingly utilize **DiskANN-style systems**, which target tens to a few hundred gigabytes of RAM strictly for index metadata and caching, while keeping compressed vector codes on NVMe storage [Vector search at 100M+ scale: IVF, HNSW, DiskANN (2026 update)](https://contentwave.net/article/enterprise-vector-search-at-100m-scale-updated-tradeoffs-june-2026). This achieves a significantly lower RAM footprint and cost per vector compared to pure in-memory graphs, though at the expense of slightly higher tail latencies [Vector search at 100M+ scale: IVF, HNSW, DiskANN (2026 update)](https://contentwave.net/article/enterprise-vector-search-at-100m-scale-updated-tradeoffs-june-2026).

