| **Flat Index** | Exhaustive brute-force scan | 100% (Exact) | Low-to-Moderate (Raw vectors only) | Extremely high (Instant inserts/deletes) | Small datasets or exact-match baselines |
| **IVF (Inverted File)** | Clustered partitions via centroids | Tunable (Moderate-to-High) | Lean (Centroids + partitioned vectors) | High (Simple incremental inserts) | Massive datasets with frequent updates |
| **HNSW** | Multi-layer navigable small world graph | Very High | High (Requires RAM for graph connections) | Costly (Rebuilding/updating graph links is heavy) | Low-latency, high-precision in-memory workloads |
| **DiskANN / Tiered** | RAM cache + NVMe storage backend | High | Low RAM footprint (NVMe-backed) | Moderate | Ultra-scale datasets (100M+ vectors) |

Caveats to note: Performance characteristics are heavily dependent on underlying hardware (RAM speed, NVMe I/O throughput), embedding dimensionality, and specific query distributions. Additionally, complex metadata filters can force databases to scan multiple partitions or increase traversal complexity, impacting search efficiency.

