## Status of the ISA Mining Code and 2026 Regulatory Roadmap

As of the conclusion of the International Seabed Authority (ISA) 31st Session in July 2026, the organization has not yet adopted the final Mining Code, formally known as the Draft Regulations for the Exploitation of Mineral Resources in the Area. According to reporting from *Ocean Mining Intel*, negotiations regarding this framework remained ongoing throughout the session, which took place from June 29 to July 31, 2026. Member states continue to work from "Revision 3" of the Further Revised Consolidated Text.

There is currently no active 2026 regulation in place for deep-sea mining exploitation. Instead, the 31st Session established a roadmap to advance the Mining Code toward the 32nd Session. The Council has requested that the Secretariat draft a roadmap for consideration at the first part of the 32nd Session, which will set out specific milestones for the timely adoption of rules, regulations, and procedures, as well as an updated list of outstanding thematic issues.

The regulatory process is characterized by the following developments:
* **Standards and Guidelines:** Member states have prioritized these as an integral part of the future Mining Code. The Legal and Technical Commission (LTC) has been tasked with reviewing existing "Phase 1" Standards and Guidelines to create a consolidated version and drafting priority items identified by the Chair.
* **Inter-sessional Work:** Informal and "Friends of the President" groups are scheduled to continue work between August 2026 and March 2027.
* **Resumption of Negotiations:** Formal negotiations on the regulatory framework are set to resume in March 2027.
* **Economic Benefit Sharing:** The Finance Committee is tasked with studying options for the equitable sharing of economic benefits, including direct distribution and a Common Heritage Fund, for presentation at the 32nd Session.
* **Existing Contracts:** Current exploration activities continue under existing exploration contracts, which were extended during the 31st Session to provide continuity while the Mining Code is finalized.

*Caveat: The source 'oceanminingintel.com' provides detailed reporting on the 31st Session; while it appears to be a specialized industry publication, it is not an official ISA press release, though it aligns with the ISA's own news summaries regarding the session's conclusion.*

