# Deep Sea Mining Regulations 2026: Comprehensive Research Report

## Executive Summary
As of late 2026, the global regulatory landscape for deep-sea mining is characterized by profound division between an international multilateral framework and a rising unilateral track led by the United States. While the International Seabed Authority (ISA) has failed to finalize its long-delayed Mining Code—leaving no active 2026 exploitation regulations in place—the United States has moved forward using domestic legislation to license activities extraterritorially in international waters. Concurrently, a broad coalition of nations is pressing for a precautionary moratorium, and private sector entities continue to advance extraction, processing, and financial frameworks amidst intense economic and environmental debate.

---
## Status of the ISA Mining Code and the Timeline for 2026 Regulations
According to specialized industry reporting from [ISA 31st Session Sets Roadmap for Mining Code Adoption at 32nd Session](https://oceanminingintel.com/news/regulation/isa-31st-session-sets-roadmap-for-mining-code-adoption-at-32nd-session/), the International Seabed Authority (ISA) concluded Part II of its 31st Session, held from June 29 to July 31, 2026, without adopting the final Mining Code (officially known as the Draft Regulations for the Exploitation of Mineral Resources in the Area). As reported by [ISA 31st Session Sets Roadmap for Mining Code Adoption at 32nd Session](https://oceanminingintel.com/news/regulation/isa-31st-session-sets-roadmap-for-mining-code-adoption-at-32nd-session/) (as of 2026-09-21, with high confidence), member states continue to negotiate using 'Revision 3' of the Further Revised Consolidated Text.

Because there is no active 2026 regulation in place, the 31st Session established a roadmap to guide the Mining Code toward the 32nd Session. The ISA Council requested that the Secretariat draft a roadmap for consideration at the first part of the 32nd Session, setting out milestones for the timely adoption of rules, regulations, and procedures, as well as an updated list of outstanding thematic issues as noted in [ISA 31st Session Sets Roadmap for Mining Code Adoption at 32nd Session](https://oceanminingintel.com/news/regulation/isa-31st-session-sets-roadmap-for-mining-code-adoption-at-32nd-session/). 

Member states have prioritized Standards and Guidelines as an integral part of the future code. The Legal and Technical Commission (LTC) has been tasked with reviewing existing 'Phase 1' Standards and Guidelines to create a consolidated version and drafting priority items identified by the Chair. Inter-sessional work, including through informal and 'Friends of the President' groups, is scheduled to run between August 2026 and March 2027, with formal negotiations on the regulatory framework set to resume in March 2027 per [ISA 31st Session Sets Roadmap for Mining Code Adoption at 32nd Session](https://oceanminingintel.com/news/regulation/isa-31st-session-sets-roadmap-for-mining-code-adoption-at-32nd-session/). Additionally, the Finance Committee is studying options for the equitable sharing of economic benefits—including direct distribution and a Common Heritage Fund—for presentation at the 32nd Session. Meanwhile, current exploration activities continue under existing exploration contracts that were extended during the 31st Session to maintain continuity.

---
## Global Regulatory Stances: Multilateral Stalemate vs. Unilateral Action
The regulatory environment is sharply divided between multilateral caution and unilateral advancement:

*   **The ISA and the Moratorium Coalition:** The ISA remains the primary governing body for international waters (the 'Area'), but stalled negotiations on environmental protection standards, benefit-sharing, and inspection powers have prevented exploitation licenses. Pressure on the ISA is amplified by a 'moratorium coalition' of at least 43 governments—including Chile, Palau, Fiji, Samoa, Vanuatu, and the Marshall Islands—that formally aligned by mid-2026 to call for a precautionary pause or moratorium on deep-sea mining activity, as documented in [ISA Deep Sea Mining Negotiations in Jamaica 2026](https://discoveryalert.com/isa-deep-sea-mining-negotiations-jamaica-2026-moratorium/) (as of 2026-07-01, high confidence).
*   **The United States Unilateral Track:** The U.S., which is not a signatory to UNCLOS, has pursued an independent regulatory path following an April 2025 executive order. Utilizing the Deep Seabed Hard Mineral Resources Act 1980 (DSHMRA), the U.S. established a consolidated application process for exploration and commercial recovery. As of March 9, 2026 (high confidence), the National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration (NOAA) determined that a consolidated application submitted by TMC USA LLC was in 'substantial compliance' with statutory requirements, permitting U.S. entities to pursue mining in international waters outside ISA oversight [Deep sea mining 2026: Regulation, geopolitics and risk](https://www.aoshearman.com/en/insights/deep-sea-mining-in-2026-regulation-geopolitics-and-the-race-for-critical-minerals).
*   **Major State Positions:** China maintains the largest exploration footprint in the ISA, sponsoring five of the 32 active ISA exploration contracts as of mid-2026 [Deep Seabed Mining Showdown: 2026 Global Alliance Shift](https://informedclearly.com/en/environment/63615/deep-seabed-mining-isa-moratorium-2026). While officially supporting multilateral governance, China prioritizes technological development and pilot trials. Pacific Island nations show internal division: Nauru strongly supports commercial mining, whereas nations like the Cook Islands, Tonga, Kiribati, and Niue engage in exploration while emphasizing science and governance, alongside those advocating outright moratoriums.
*   **Private Sector and Infrastructure:** Commercial interest is advancing through firms such as The Metals Company (TMC), Deep Sea Rare Minerals (DSRM), and American Ocean Minerals Corporation (AOMC). Infrastructure is also expanding, with companies like Glomar Minerals and Cobalt Blue Holdings planning U.S.-based refineries for polymetallic nodules to secure battery-metal supply chains [Deep sea mining 2026: Regulation, geopolitics and risk](https://www.aoshearman.com/en/insights/deep-sea-mining-in-2026-regulation-geopolitics-and-the-race-for-critical-minerals).

---
## Economic and Environmental Drivers and Policy Flashpoints
The 2026 regulatory debate is propelled by opposing economic and environmental pressures:

*   **Pro-Regulation and Decarbonization Momentum:** Proponents emphasize global momentum toward mandatory climate disclosures, carbon pricing, sustainable finance initiatives, and climate risk management requirements to improve transparency, investor confidence, and market efficiency [Climate Policy Landscape 2026: Global Regulatory Trends](https://www.aranca.com/knowledge-library/articles/business-research/climate-policy-landscape-2026-global-regulatory-trends). In the context of seabed mining, environmental advocates warn against severe risks such as sediment plumes, noise pollution, and deep-sea ecosystem degradation.
*   **Deregulation and Industrial Competitiveness:** Conversely, critics of heavy environmental regulation—particularly within the U.S. executive branch and Congress—focus on compliance costs, industrial competitiveness, and consumer choice. Arguments are raised that agencies like the EPA have overstepped statutory authority regarding greenhouse gas emissions [What’s coming in 2026 for energy and environmental policy](https://cei.org/blog/whats-coming-in-2026-for-energy-and-environmental-policy/).
*   **Key Policy Flashpoints:** Flashpoints in 2026 include the reconsideration of the 2009 EPA endangerment finding, potential repeal of greenhouse gas standards for power plants, and revision of the 'Waters of the United States' (WOTUS) definition following *Sackett v. EPA*. Additionally, a proposed 2026–2031 National Outer Continental Shelf Oil and Gas Leasing Program could encompass up to 34 lease sales covering approximately 1.27 billion acres [What’s coming in 2026 for energy and environmental policy](https://cei.org/blog/whats-coming-in-2026-for-energy-and-environmental-policy/). Businesses continue to grapple with jurisdictional fragmentation, data availability issues, and supply chain sustainability demands [Climate Policy Landscape 2026: Global Regulatory Trends](https://www.aranca.com/knowledge-library/articles/business-research/climate-policy-landscape-2026-global-regulatory-trends).

