## Keyboard-Only Testing in Web Accessibility (2026 Context)

In the context of software testing and web accessibility, 'keyboard-only testing' is defined as the manual practice of evaluating a website, web application, or digital product's usability and compliance entirely without a mouse or touchscreen, using only keyboard inputs such as the Tab, Shift+Tab, Enter, Spacebar, and arrow keys ([DubBot](https://dubbot.com/dubblog/2026/why-keyboard-only-testing-should-be-your-first-accessibility-check.html)). 

As highlighted in 2026 industry discussions—such as Greg Gibson’s presentation 'Testing Web Experiences with Your Keyboard' featured at axe-con in February 2026—keyboard-only testing is considered a fundamental, low-barrier, and highly revealing initial accessibility check ([DubBot](https://dubbot.com/dubblog/2026/why-keyboard-only-testing-should-be-your-first-accessibility-check.html)). It directly targets WCAG requirements (specifically WCAG 2.1 Level A Success Criterion 2.1.1: Keyboard) to ensure that all interactive elements, including links, buttons, form controls, modals, and tooltips, can be reached, operated, and exited without causing keyboard traps ([DubBot](https://dubbot.com/dubblog/2026/why-keyboard-only-testing-should-be-your-first-accessibility-check.html)).

### Key Aspects and Findings
- **Simplicity and Efficacy:** Keyboard-only testing is recognized as one of the simplest and most revealing ways to evaluate accessibility and usability without a mouse or touchscreen ([DubBot](https://dubbot.com/dubblog/2026/why-keyboard-only-testing-should-be-your-first-accessibility-check.html)).
- **Uncovering Hidden Elements:** Visible focus styles and proper keyboard navigation help uncover hidden inline links and styling or contrast issues that affect users with color vision deficiencies ([DubBot](https://dubbot.com/dubblog/2026/why-keyboard-only-testing-should-be-your-first-accessibility-check.html)). For example, during Greg Gibson’s presentation at axe-con in February 2026, tabbing through a paragraph revealed three otherwise non-detectable links upon receiving focus ([DubBot](https://dubbot.com/dubblog/2026/why-keyboard-only-testing-should-be-your-first-accessibility-check.html)).
- **Focus Management:** Proper modal behavior dictates that when a user closes a modal via its close button, keyboard focus must return to the original trigger element to maintain a sense of place and context ([DubBot](https://dubbot.com/dubblog/2026/why-keyboard-only-testing-should-be-your-first-accessibility-check.html)).

