## 2026 Arctic Sea Ice Extent Trends and Projections

In 2026, Arctic sea ice reached an annual minimum extent of 4.60 million square kilometers (1.78 million square miles) on September 12, 2026, according to the National Snow and Ice Data Center (NSIDC). This figure ties the 2026 minimum with the years 2008, 2010, and 2025 for the tenth lowest extent in the satellite record, which spans nearly 48 years since late 1978. Consistent with long-term observations, the past 20 years (2007–2026) have contained the 20 lowest annual Arctic sea ice minimum extents recorded since continuous satellite monitoring began.

Winter trends in 2026 marked a reversal of previous patterns that had suggested a period of temporary stabilization. Research published in *PNAS* indicates that exceptionally low ice extents during the 2025 and 2026 winters caused sea-ice extent during the growth and peak phases to return to record lows. Consequently, the 20-year decline trends for Arctic sea ice have become statistically significant once again.

Regarding seasonal forecasting, mid-2026 projections estimated an Arctic sea-ice extent of approximately 4.49 million square kilometers for mid-September. This projection, cited in research from *Nature*, slightly underestimated the final observed minimum of 4.60 million square kilometers. These mid-season forecasts also predicted the opening of the Northern Sea Route along the Russian coast in late August and the route along the Canadian coast (excluding the archipelago) in late July.

### Caveats
The 4.49 million square kilometer figure provided by the *Nature* source is a mid-season projection, which resulted in a slight underestimation of the final observed minimum of 4.60 million square kilometers.

