## Vertical Farming Energy Economics and Crop Yields: 2026 Report

### Energy Economics and Operational Expenditure
As of 2026, the vertical farming industry has transitioned from a growth-at-all-costs model to one focused on operational discipline. Electricity is the primary variable cost, typically accounting for 25% to over 40% of total operating expenditure (OpEx), according to *Advanced Autoponics*. Within this power draw, LED lighting represents 50% to 65% of consumption, while HVAC and climate control account for 20% to 30% (*Agro Reality*).

To remain viable, leading operators now target an energy efficiency benchmark of 11–15 kWh per kilogram of salable product, a significant improvement over older facilities that can exceed 40 kWh/kg (*Beyond Tomorrow*). Competitive survivors are aiming for electricity costs below $0.08 per kWh, achieved through a combination of agricultural utility rates, on-site renewable generation with battery storage, and industrial waste-heat partnerships (*Beyond Tomorrow*).

### Technological Advancements in Efficiency
Technological focus has shifted toward "closed-loop" intelligent control systems. Strategies such as automated Daily Light Integral (DLI) management and variable-speed ventilation are reported to unlock energy savings of 15% to 30% compared to static, open-loop schedules (*Advanced Autoponics*). 

Specific research on *Lactuca sativa* (lettuce) indicates that dynamic lighting—specifically hourly alternating reductions of blue light by 25% to 38%—can increase fresh marketable yield by 50% to 60%, improve light-energy use efficiency by up to 63%, and reduce overall artificial lighting energy costs by 40% (*Frontiers in Plant Science*). Furthermore, optimal productivity for lettuce is supported by CO2 fertilization saturating at approximately 900 ppm and a temperature of 24 °C (*ScienceDirect*).

### Crop Yield Benchmarks
Modern vertical farms utilize multi-tiered architectures (3 to 6+ layers) to achieve significant yield multipliers over open-field agriculture:
*   **Leafy Greens (Lettuce/Spinach):** 50 to 120 kg per square meter annually (*Farmonaut*).
*   **Herbs (Basil/Cilantro):** 30 to 80 kg per square meter annually (*Frontiers in Plant Science*).
*   **Microgreens:** 90 to 180 kg per square meter annually (*Frontiers in Plant Science*).
*   **Fruit/High-Value Crops (Strawberries/Tomatoes):** 15 to 40 kg per square meter annually (*Frontiers in Plant Science*).

These systems also provide substantial resource efficiency, using 90% to 95% less water than traditional soil farming and reducing pesticide residue use by 80% to 90% (*World Metrics*).

### Market Outlook and Economic Viability
The industry is currently valued at approximately USD 9.5 billion to 11.66 billion for 2026, with projections reaching between USD 45.4 billion and nearly USD 68 billion by the mid-2030s (*Research and Markets*; *Precedence Research*). Another estimate suggests global revenues could hit USD 20.5 billion by 2031 with a CAGR of 24% (*Just Vertical*). 

Following the contraction of high-profile operators like Bowery, institutional investors now prioritize facilities with secured off-take agreements and clear profitability gates (*Beyond Tomorrow*; *Just Vertical*). Successful commercial models are moving away from single-crop bulk production toward diversified portfolios of 3 to 5 high-value crops to buffer against market volatility (*Just Vertical*).

