## Hierarchical Navigable Small World (HNSW) Indexing and Trade-offs

Hierarchical Navigable Small World (HNSW) is an approximate nearest neighbor (ANN) search algorithm that leverages a multi-layered graph structure to facilitate high-performance vector retrieval. According to [Pinecone](https://www.pinecone.io/learn/series/faiss/hnsw/), the algorithm integrates two core concepts: probability skip lists for hierarchical traversal and Navigable Small World (NSW) graphs for greedy routing. The index is organized into layers where the top layers contain sparse, long-range links for rapid navigation, and lower layers contain dense, short-range links for fine-grained accuracy. During a search, the algorithm begins at the highest layer, greedily traversing nodes to identify the closest neighbor, and descends through the layers until it reaches the base layer (Layer 0), which contains all data points.

The construction of the index involves inserting vectors one by one, with a probability function determining the maximum layer for each node to ensure most nodes reside in lower layers. Practical performance and resource utilization are governed by specific parameters:

*   **M (Number of links):** This parameter defines the number of established bidirectional links for every new element. Increasing M improves recall but results in significantly higher memory consumption, as each node must store more links.
*   **efConstruction:** This parameter sets the size of the dynamic candidate list during the construction phase. A higher value creates a higher-quality graph, which improves search recall but increases the time required to build the index.
*   **efSearch:** This parameter controls the size of the dynamic candidate list during the search phase. Increasing this value improves recall but increases search latency.

HNSW is generally favored for applications requiring low latency and high accuracy. However, it requires more memory than tree-based or hash-based alternatives due to the overhead of storing graph links.

