FINAL: This report examines the performance, accuracy, and memory trade-offs associated with Flat, Inverted File (IVF), and Hierarchical Navigable Small World (HNSW) vector search indexes. It details how each structural approach balances computational cost against recall, providing a guide for selecting the appropriate index based on specific application requirements.

## Vector Index Trade-offs: Flat, IVF, and HNSW

Vector search indexes trade off performance (latency and build time), accuracy (recall), and memory footprint based on their structural approach to approximate nearest neighbor (ANN) search, as detailed in [Vector Index Trade-offs: Flat vs IVF vs HNSW, and When Quantization](https://cloudandsre.com/blog/vector-index-hnsw-vs-ivf/).

### Flat (Brute-Force) Indexes
Flat indexes compare the query vector against every vector in the corpus. They provide exact accuracy with a recall of 1.0 (by construction, with no quality question to investigate), zero build time, instant updates, and trivial parallelization. However, performance degrades linearly with dataset size ($O(n \cdot d)$ per query), making it unsuitable for large-scale corpora where low latency is required. It serves as an essential ground-truth baseline.

### Inverted File (IVF) Indexes
Inverted File (IVF) indexes cluster vectors into $n_{list}$ centroids using $k$-means. At query time, they restrict the search to the $n_{probe}$ centroids closest to the query, providing a direct, tunable trade-off between speed and recall via the $n_{probe}$ parameter. For example, with $n_{list} = 4096$ and $n_{probe} = 16$, you scan roughly $16/4096$ of the corpus—about $0.4\%$ of the vectors, yielding a $\sim 250\times$ reduction in comparisons. IVF's memory overhead is extremely low, consisting of centroids and simple list assignments. However, IVF requires up-front training, suffers from corpus drift (requiring periodic retraining as data distribution shifts), and exhibits boundary problems where true neighbors across cluster edges are missed.

### Hierarchical Navigable Small World (HNSW) Indexes
Hierarchical Navigable Small World (HNSW) indexes build a hierarchy of proximity graphs where upper layers act as coarse "motorways" and the base layer holds all vectors. Search scales logarithmically with corpus size $n$, offering excellent recall-latency trade-offs (e.g., high recall at low latencies). Key parameters include $M$ (links per node per layer, dictating memory and recall) and $ef$ (query-time candidate list size). The primary disadvantage of HNSW is significant memory overhead—storing both the full vectors and the proximity graph in RAM, or search degrades catastrophically when it starts hitting disk—alongside heavy build times and expensive soft deletes (tombstones) under high churn workloads.

Sources:
- [Vector Index Trade-offs: Flat vs IVF vs HNSW, and When Quantization](https://cloudandsre.com/blog/vector-index-hnsw-vs-ivf/)

