## Performance and Latency Trade-offs

Vector indexing strategies—Flat, IVF, and HNSW—present distinct trade-offs regarding query latency, throughput, and scalability. These performance metrics are heavily influenced by hardware specifications, such as CPU cores, instruction sets, and memory bandwidth, as well as the dimensionality of the embedding models used.

### Flat Indexing: The Exact Baseline
Flat indexing operates via exhaustive brute-force distance calculation, resulting in an O(n * d) complexity. Because it performs a sequential scan over all vectors, it guarantees 100% recall (recall@10 = 1.0). However, this exactness comes at a significant performance cost as datasets grow. On a 10-million-vector dataset with 768-dimensional vectors, Flat indexing records a p95 query latency of 44.7 ms and a CPU throughput of 232 queries per second (QPS) using 8 threads. Due to this linear scaling, Flat indexing is generally considered viable only for datasets containing fewer than 500,000 to 1 million vectors.

### IVF (Inverted File) Indexing: Tunable Efficiency
IVF indexing partitions the vector space into Voronoi cells using k-means centroids during a mandatory training phase. At query time, the system probes a subset of inverted lists defined by the `nprobe` parameter. This allows operators to trade off recall for speed. In FAISS benchmarks (10M vectors, 768-dim, nlist=2048, nprobe=64), IVF achieves a p50 latency of 0.62 ms, a p95 of 0.83 ms, and a p99 of 1.12 ms, with a recall@10 of 0.962. CPU throughput for this configuration reaches approximately 9,800 QPS. When utilizing GPU-batching (NVIDIA A10G, batch size 128), IVF throughput can scale to 40,100 QPS with a batch p95 latency of 2.1 ms. In PostgreSQL/pgvector environments with 1M vectors, IVFFlat (lists=100, probes=10) yields 680 QPS with an average latency of 94 ms and a p99 of 210 ms. A primary drawback of IVF is its sensitivity to the initial build; if the data distribution drifts, the index requires periodic reindexing to maintain performance.

### HNSW (Hierarchical Navigable Small World): High-Performance Graphs
HNSW utilizes a multi-layer graph structure where sparse upper layers facilitate long-range navigation and dense bottom layers handle precise local search, achieving O(log n) query complexity. In 10M vector FAISS benchmarks (M=32, efConstruction=200, efSearch=64), HNSW delivers a p50 latency of 0.31 ms, a p95 of 0.42 ms, and a p99 of 0.56 ms, maintaining a recall@10 of 0.964. Peak CPU throughput reaches 11,200 QPS across 8 threads. In PostgreSQL 16/pgvector benchmarks using 1M vectors, HNSW (m=16, ef_construction=200, ef_search=40) achieves 2,300 QPS—a 3.4x throughput advantage over IVFFlat—with a p99 latency of 48 ms. HNSW is particularly advantageous for streaming workloads because it absorbs continuous inserts without requiring full index rebuilds. However, this performance comes with a higher memory footprint (approximately 2.5x to 4x the raw vector size, or 12.8 GB vs. 4.2 GB for 10M vectors) and longer build times compared to IVF.

### Comparative Performance Summary

| Index Type | Latency (p95, 10M vectors) | Throughput (CPU) | Recall@10 | Memory Usage (10M vectors) |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Flat** | 44.7 ms | 232 QPS | 1.0 | Raw size |
| **IVF** | 0.83 ms | 9,800 QPS | 0.962 | ~4.2 GB |
| **HNSW** | 0.42 ms | 11,200 QPS | 0.964 | ~12.8 GB |

*Note: Benchmarks reflect 768-dimensional float32 vectors. Performance figures are based on data as of 2026-03-01 and 2026-09-19.*

