## Performance Comparison: Latency and Throughput

Vector indexing strategies define the trade-offs between query latency, throughput, and operational resilience. The three foundational approaches—Flat, IVF, and HNSW—exhibit distinct performance profiles when handling large-scale vector datasets.

### Flat Indexing
Flat indexing utilizes an exact brute-force sequential scan (O(n * d) complexity). While it guarantees 100% recall (recall@10 = 1.0), it is limited by its linear scaling. On a 10-million-vector dataset (768-dimensional), Flat records a p95 query latency of 44.7 ms and a CPU throughput of 232 queries per second (QPS) using 8 threads. In PostgreSQL environments, this throughput drops to 21 QPS. Due to these constraints, Flat is generally only viable for real-time applications with datasets under 500,000 vectors.

### IVF (Inverted File) Indexing
IVF partitions vector space into Voronoi cells using k-means centroids, probing a subset of these lists at query time. In FAISS benchmarks with 10 million vectors (nlist=2048, nprobe=64), IVF achieves a p50 latency of 0.62 ms, p95 of 0.83 ms, and p99 of 1.12 ms, with a recall@10 of 0.962 and CPU throughput of 9,800 QPS. In PostgreSQL/pgvector benchmarks with 1 million vectors (lists=100, probes=10), IVFFlat yields 680 QPS with an average latency of 94 ms and a p99 of 210 ms. When accelerated via GPU-batching on an A10G (batch size 128), IVF throughput scales significantly to 40,100 QPS. Primary limitations include sensitivity to data drift, which necessitates periodic reindexing, and higher latency variance under concurrent load.

### HNSW (Hierarchical Navigable Small World) Indexing
HNSW utilizes a multi-layered graph structure to achieve O(log n) query complexity. In 10 million vector FAISS benchmarks (M=32, efConstruction=200, efSearch=64), HNSW delivers a p50 of 0.31 ms, p95 of 0.42 ms, and p99 of 0.56 ms, with a recall@10 of 0.964 and peak CPU throughput of 11,200 QPS. Under PostgreSQL 16 / pgvector benchmarks with 1 million vectors (m=16, ef_construction=200, ef_search=40), HNSW achieves 2,300 QPS—a 3.4x throughput improvement over IVFFlat—with a p99 latency of 48 ms. HNSW maintains consistent sub-50 ms tail latencies and supports streaming inserts without requiring full index rebuilds. However, it is memory-intensive, consuming 2.5x to 4x more memory than raw vector storage (e.g., 12.8 GB vs. 4.2 GB for 10 million vectors), and requires longer build times.

### Summary of Performance Metrics (10M Vectors)

| Index Type | p95 Latency | Throughput (CPU) | Recall@10 |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Flat** | 44.7 ms | 232 QPS | 1.0 |
| **IVF** | 0.83 ms | 9,800 QPS | 0.962 |
