## Comparative Analysis of IVF and HNSW Vector Indexes

The following table outlines the primary trade-offs between Inverted File Index (IVF) and Hierarchical Navigable Small World (HNSW) algorithms, which are commonly utilized for approximate nearest neighbor search in high-dimensional vector spaces.

| Feature | IVF (Inverted File Index) | HNSW (Hierarchical Navigable Small World) |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Core Mechanism** | Partitions vector space into Voronoi cells; searches only relevant clusters. | Constructs a multi-layered graph where nodes are connected based on proximity. |
| **Search Speed** | Moderate; depends on the number of clusters (nlist) and probes (nprobe). | Very fast; logarithmic complexity due to the hierarchical graph structure. |
| **Memory Usage** | Relatively low; stores cluster centroids and vector assignments. | High; requires significant memory to store the multi-layered graph edges. |
| **Build Time** | Faster; involves clustering (e.g., K-means) of the dataset. | Slower; requires building a complex graph structure during indexing. |
| **Recall** | Can be lower if the number of probes is insufficient for the query. | Generally higher; provides excellent recall even at high speeds. |
| **Dynamic Updates** | Easier to manage; clusters can be updated or re-trained. | Difficult; adding or removing nodes requires complex graph re-linking. |

