## PostgreSQL vs. MySQL Comparison

MySQL is a purely relational database management system often favored for read-heavy web applications due to its lighter overhead, whereas PostgreSQL is an object-relational database management system that provides superior ACID compliance and advanced feature support—such as materialized views and complex concurrency control—making it better suited for enterprise-level applications with frequent, complex write operations (Source: [What’s the Difference Between MySQL and PostgreSQL?](https://aws.amazon.com/compare/the-difference-between-mysql-vs-postgresql/)).

Sources:
- [What’s the Difference Between MySQL and PostgreSQL?](https://aws.amazon.com/compare/the-difference-between-mysql-vs-postgresql/)

MySQL and PostgreSQL are both popular open-source relational database management systems that utilize SQL, but they differ fundamentally in their philosophies, architectures, and feature sets [What’s the Difference Between MySQL and PostgreSQL?](https://aws.amazon.com/compare/the-difference-between-mysql-vs-postgresql/). 

At a foundational level, MySQL is a purely relational database, whereas PostgreSQL is an object-relational database, which allows data to be stored as objects with properties while supporting parent-child relationships and inheritance [What’s the Difference Between MySQL and PostgreSQL?](https://aws.amazon.com/compare/the-difference-between-mysql-vs-postgresql/). Regarding data types, MySQL supports numeric, character, date and time, spatial, and JSON types, while PostgreSQL extends its support to geometric, enumerated, network address, arrays, ranges, XML, hstore, and composite types [What’s the Difference Between MySQL and PostgreSQL?](https://aws.amazon.com/compare/the-difference-between-mysql-vs-postgresql/).

ACID compliance and concurrency control also vary by design. MySQL offers ACID compliance only when you use it with InnoDB and NDB Cluster storage engines or software modules, whereas PostgreSQL is fully ACID compliant in all configurations [What’s the Difference Between MySQL and PostgreSQL?](https://aws.amazon.com/compare/the-difference-between-mysql-vs-postgresql/). Furthermore, MySQL relies on specific storage engines for its multiversion concurrency control (MVCC), while PostgreSQL supports MVCC natively across all configurations [What’s the Difference Between MySQL and PostgreSQL?](https://aws.amazon.com/compare/the-difference-between-mysql-vs-postgresql/). For indexing, MySQL supports B-tree and R-tree indexing, compared to PostgreSQL's broader array of options that include trees, expression indexes, partial indexes, and hash indexes [What’s the Difference Between MySQL and PostgreSQL?](https://aws.amazon.com/compare/the-difference-between-mysql-vs-postgresql/).

Advanced features such as views, triggers, and stored procedures differ significantly between the two systems. While MySQL supports standard views, PostgreSQL offers advanced view options such as materialized views—allowing users to precompute values in advance, such as the total value of all orders over a given period [What’s the Difference Between MySQL and PostgreSQL?](https://aws.amazon.com/compare/the-difference-between-mysql-vs-postgresql/). Additionally, MySQL is limited to AFTER and BEFORE triggers for INSERT, UPDATE, and DELETE, whereas PostgreSQL supports the INSTEAD OF trigger to run complex SQL statements using functions [What’s the Difference Between MySQL and PostgreSQL?](https://aws.amazon.com/compare/the-difference-between-mysql-vs-postgresql/). Both systems support stored procedures, though PostgreSQL also allows them to be written in languages other than SQL [What’s the Difference Between MySQL and PostgreSQL?](https://aws.amazon.com/compare/the-difference-between-mysql-vs-postgresql/).

In terms of performance and operational characteristics, PostgreSQL is better suited for enterprise-level applications with frequent write operations and complex queries due to its advanced MVCC implementation and robust handling of concurrent updates [What’s the Difference Between MySQL and PostgreSQL?](https://aws.amazon.com/compare/the-difference-between-mysql-vs-postgresql/). Conversely, MySQL often outperforms PostgreSQL in read-heavy scenarios, web applications, and simpler use cases thanks to its lighter memory overhead, simpler process management, and more accessible learning curve for beginners [What’s the Difference Between MySQL and PostgreSQL?](https://aws.amazon.com/compare/the-difference-between-mysql-vs-postgresql/).

Sources:
- [What’s the Difference Between MySQL and PostgreSQL?](https://aws.amazon.com/compare/the-difference-between-mysql-vs-postgresql/)
